[Event "Ruy Lopez: Closed main line"]

{The Spanish torture. White pressures e5 indirectly by attacking its defender on c6, builds with c3/d4 and keeps a long-term edge in space.}
1. e4 {[%csl Gd5,Gf5] Grabs the centre and frees the queen and bishop.}
1... e5 {Black stakes an equal claim to the centre. The pawn on e5 controls d4 and f4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8 and the bishop on f8.}
2. Nf3 {[%cal Gf3e5] Develops with an attack on e5. The knight on f3 controls d4 and eyes the pawn on e5. That pawn is currently undefended.}
2... Nc6 {Defends e5 and develops. The knight on c6 controls d4 and supports the pawn on e5.}
3. Bb5 {[%cal Gb5c6] The Ruy Lopez: the bishop attacks the knight that guards e5.}
3... a6 {The Morphy Defence: Black asks the bishop at once.}
4. Ba4 {Keeps the pin-like pressure on c6. Taking on c6 now is the Exchange variation, a different plan.}
4... Nf6 {[%cal Gf6e4] Develops and counterattacks e4. The knight on f6 controls d5 and eyes the pawn on e4. That pawn is currently undefended.}
5. O-O {King safety first. e4 is "poisoned" for now because Re1 will regain it.}
5... Be7 {The Closed Ruy: Black prepares to castle and keeps the e5 pawn solid.}
6. Re1 {[%csl Ge4] Defends e4 so that Bxc6 followed by Nxe5 becomes a real threat.}
6... b5 {[%cal Rb5a4] Kicks the bishop to break the pressure on c6. It concedes some queenside looseness (a4 levers later).}
7. Bb3 {[%cal Gb3f7] The bishop now eyes f7 along the a2–g8 diagonal.}
7... d6 {Shores up e5 and opens the c8-bishop. Supports the centre: the pawn on d6 controls c5 and supports the pawn on e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8.}
8. c3 {[%csl Gd4] Prepares d4 and gives the bishop a retreat to c2.}
8... O-O {Black castles. White must now stop ...Bg4, which would pin the f3-knight against the queen and pressure d4.}
9. h3 {[%csl Gg4] The key quiet move: no ...Bg4, so d4 can come without being undermined.}
9... Na5 {The Chigorin: the knight hits the b3-bishop and frees c7 for ...c5.}
 (9... Bb7 {The Zaitsev: the bishop eyes e4 and Black will play ...Re8 and ...Bf8.}
10. d4 {[%csl Gd4,Ge4] White builds the ideal centre now that ...Bg4 is ruled out.}
10... Re8 {Adds pressure to e4 and prepares ...Bf8. The rook goes to e8.}
11. Nbd2 {Defends e4 and heads for f1–g3. Develops the knight to d2, where it supports the pawn on e4.}
11... Bf8 {Re-routes the bishop to support the e5 point and the kingside.}
)
 (9... Nb8 {The Breyer: the knight re-routes to d7 to support e5 and free the c-pawn.}
10. d4 {Takes the centre. The pawn on d4 controls c5 and eyes the pawn on e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
10... Nbd7 {Completes the Breyer manoeuvre. Develops the knight to d7, where it supports the pawn on e5.}
11. Nbd2 {The standard knight route via f1 to g3. Develops the knight to d2, where it supports the pawn on e4.}
11... Bb7 {Pressure on e4. Fianchettoes the bishop on b7, where it rakes the long diagonal through c6, d5 and e4.}
12. Bc2 {Keeps the bishop on the b1–h7 diagonal, eyeing the kingside.}
)
10. Bc2 {The bishop stays on the long diagonal aiming at h7.}
10... c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Black gains queenside space and pressures d4.}
11. d4 {[%csl Gd4,Ge4] Strikes the centre: the classic Ruy Lopez tension.}
11... Qc7 {Supports e5 and the c-file. The queen goes to c7.}
12. Nbd2 {The knight heads to f1 and g3, where it eyes f5.} *

[Event "Ruy Lopez: Open & Anti-Marshall"]

{Two important tries for Black: grabbing e4 (the Open Ruy) and preparing the Marshall Gambit with ...O-O. Against the Marshall White sidesteps with a4.}
1. e4 {Grabs the centre. The pawn on e4 controls d5 and f5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1 and the bishop on f1.}
1... e5 {Classical reply. Claims the centre: the pawn on e5 controls d4 and f4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8 and the bishop on f8.}
2. Nf3 {Attacks e5. Develops the knight to f3, where it controls d4 and eyes the pawn on e5. That pawn is currently undefended.}
2... Nc6 {Develops the knight to c6, where it controls d4 and supports the pawn on e5.}
3. Bb5 {The Spanish. Develops the bishop to b5, where it controls c4 and eyes the knight on c6. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
3... a6 {Morphy Defence. The pawn on a6 eyes the bishop on b5. That bishop is currently undefended.}
4. Ba4 {Keeps the pressure. Moves the bishop out of attack to a4.}
4... Nf6 {Counterattack on e4. Develops the knight to f6, where it controls d5 and eyes the pawn on e4. That pawn is currently undefended.}
5. O-O {Castles; e4 can be regained. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
5... Nxe4 {The Open Ruy: Black takes and accepts an open game.}
 (5... Be7 {Heading for the Closed lines. Develops the bishop to e7, where it controls d6 and supports the knight on f6. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
6. Re1 {Defends e4 and renews the threat on e5. The rook goes to e1 and supports the pawn on e4.}
6... b5 {Breaks the pin. Gains space on the queenside; the pawn on b5 takes a4 and c4 away from the opponent. It attacks the bishop on a4, which is undefended.}
7. Bb3 {Eyes f7. Moves the bishop out of attack to b3 and controls d5.}
7... O-O {Black castles, hoping for 8.c3 d5!? (the Marshall Gambit).}
8. a4 {[%cal Ga4b5] The Anti-Marshall: White hits b5 before Black can sacrifice d5.}
8... b4 {Gains space and keeps the queenside closed. The pawn on b4 controls a3 and c3.}
 (8... Bb7 {Develops and keeps b5 defended. Fianchettoes the bishop on b7, where it rakes the long diagonal through c6.}
9. d3 {Solid: no Marshall, and e4 is defended for good.}
9... d6 {Supports e5. Supports the centre: the pawn on d6 controls c5 and supports the pawn on e5.}
10. Nbd2 {The knight heads for f1–g3 or c4. Develops the knight to d2, where it supports the pawn on e4.}
)
9. d3 {Calm and strong: e4 is held and the Marshall is off.}
9... d6 {Solid support for e5. Supports the centre: the pawn on d6 controls c5 and supports the pawn on e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8.}
10. a5 {[%csl Gb6] Fixes the queenside and gives the bishop a5 later if needed.}
)
6. d4 {[%csl Ge5] Opens the centre immediately: White's pieces are better placed to exploit it.}
6... b5 {Black must deal with Bb5 pins and threats on e5.}
7. Bb3 {Back to the strong diagonal. Moves the bishop out of attack to b3 and controls d5.}
7... d5 {[%csl Gd5] Supports the advanced knight on e4. Claims the centre: the pawn on d5 controls c4 and supports the knight on e4. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8 and the queen on d8.}
8. dxe5 {Gains space. The e5 pawn cramps Black's kingside.}
8... Be6 {Protects d5. Develops the bishop to e6, where it supports the pawn on d5.}
9. c3 {[%csl Gc2] Gives the bishop c2 and prepares Nbd2 to challenge e4.}
9... Be7 {Develops the bishop to e7, where it controls f6 and d6. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
10. Nbd2 {[%cal Gd2e4] Challenges the e4 knight. Develops the knight to d2, where it eyes the knight on e4.} *

[Event "Ruy Lopez: vs the Berlin & early deviations"]

{The Berlin (3...Nf6) is the main drawing weapon at top level. A practical approach is 4.d3, keeping pieces on the board. Also: how to handle 3...a6 4.Ba4 d6.}
1. e4 {Grabs the centre. The pawn on e4 controls d5 and f5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1 and the bishop on f1.}
1... e5 {Claims the centre: the pawn on e5 controls d4 and f4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8 and the bishop on f8.}
2. Nf3 {Attacks e5. Develops the knight to f3, where it controls d4 and eyes the pawn on e5. That pawn is currently undefended.}
2... Nc6 {Develops the knight to c6, where it controls d4 and supports the pawn on e5.}
3. Bb5 {The Spanish. Develops the bishop to b5, where it controls c4 and eyes the knight on c6. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
3... Nf6 {The Berlin: Black counterattacks e4 at once. Develops the knight to f6, where it controls d5 and eyes the pawn on e4. That pawn is currently undefended.}
 (3... a6 {Morphy Defence. The pawn on a6 eyes the bishop on b5. That bishop is currently undefended.}
4. Ba4 {Keeps the tension. Moves the bishop out of attack to a4.}
4... d6 {The Modern Steinitz: Black solidifies e5 before ...Nf6.}
5. c3 {[%csl Gd4] Prepares d4 and keeps a retreat for the bishop.}
5... Bd7 {Breaks the pin and unpins the c6-knight. Develops the bishop to d7, where it controls e6 and f5.}
6. d4 {[%csl Gd4,Ge4] Builds the big centre. The pawn on d4 controls c5 and eyes the pawn on e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
6... Nf6 {Develops and hits e4. The knight on f6 controls d5 and eyes the pawn on e4. That pawn is currently undefended.}
7. O-O {Castles. e4 is safe for now because ...Nxe4 runs into Re1 and d5.}
)
4. d3 {[%csl Ge4] The Anti-Berlin: White protects e4 and avoids the endgame.}
4... Bc5 {Active: the bishop targets f2. Develops the bishop to c5, where it controls d4. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
5. c3 {[%csl Gd4] Prepares d4 and gives the b5-bishop a retreat.}
5... O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
6. O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
6... d6 {Supports the centre: the pawn on d6 supports the bishop on c5 and the pawn on e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8.}
7. h3 {[%csl Gg4] Stops ...Bg4. The pawn on h3 controls g4.}
7... a6 {Asks the bishop. The pawn on a6 eyes the bishop on b5. That bishop is currently undefended.}
8. Ba4 {Keeps the bishop on the a4–e8 diagonal, heading to c2 later.} *
